  "text": "Certainly! You're asking to write a function `word_histogram(sentence)` that takes a string input and returns a dictionary where each **word** is a key, and its **value** is the number of times it appears in the string. \n\n### Key Requirements:\n- Ignore **case** (i.e., treat \"Hello\" and \"hello\" as the same word).\n- Ignore **punctuation** (e.g., commas, periods, exclamation marks).\n\n---\n\n### Plan:\n1. Convert the entire string to **lowercase**.\n2. **Remove punctuation**.\n3. **Split** the string into words.\n4. Use a **dictionary** or `collections.defaultdict` to count word frequencies.\n\n---\n\n### \u2705 Example:\n```python\nword_histogram(\"Hello, world! Hello.\") == {\"hello\": 2, \"world\": 1}\n```\n\n---\n\n### \u2705 Final Code:\n\n```python\nimport string\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\ndef word_histogram(sentence):\n    # Convert to lowercase\n    sentence = sentence.lower()\n    \n    # Remove punctuation\n    punctuations = string.punctuation\n    sentence = ''.join(char for char in sentence if char not in punctuations)\n    \n    # Split into words\n    words = sentence.split()\n    \n    # Count word frequencies\n    histogram = defaultdict(int)\n    for word in words:\n        histogram[word] += 1\n    \n    return dict(histogram)\n```\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udca1 How it works:\n- `string.punctuation` gives all punctuation (e.g., `!\"#$%&'()*+,-./:;<=>?@[\\]^_`{|}~`).\n- We filter out punctuation and split the string into individual words.\n- We use `collections.defaultdict(int)` to make it easy to count frequencies.\n\n---\n\n### \u2705 Unit Tests:\n```python\nassert word_histogram(\"Hello, world! Hello.\") == {\"hello\": 2, \"world\": 1}\nassert word_histogram(\"Python is great. Python is fun!\") == {\"python\": 2, \"is\": 2, \"great\": 1, \"fun\": 1}\nassert word_histogram(\"Test, test, TEST.\") == {\"test\": 3}\n```\n\nThese should all pass with the above code.\n\nLet me know if you'd like a version without `collections.defaultdict` or a one-liner using `Counter` from `collections`!",
